[ ] Specify or forbid a plan_path shared by several workspaces $each workspace sees the other's collections as unowned-plans-collection, quarantines them, and doctor fix may offer to delete them; every workspace also owns next and inbox collections, so names collide too. Found 2026-09-15. DEFERRED 2026-09-18, not decided: this needs investigation before a call is safe — does any real config actually share a plan_path across two clearhead workspaces today (additional_workspaces entries are separate repos with their own plans/ dirs; when would they collide)? Only once that's confirmed is it safe to decide forbid (validate at load, error out) vs support (design real cross-workspace collection ownership/namespacing) and write it into specifications/workspace.md. Out of scope for tonight's platform+specifications overnight pass; needs a clearhead-core investigation session first.$ ^2026-09-15T01:24 #01a0a42b-3be7-7691-bcbf-366e1fd87a77
[ ] Tidy the overnight run review nits $DECIDED 2026-09-23: tolerate file-based partial failure; do not simulate a database transaction or promise rollback across charter Markdown and calendar displayname. The charter document is authoritative. If its write commits and displayname refresh fails, report the charter as committed with derived metadata incomplete and an actionable repair path. Derive the desired displayname from current charter source; make drift detectable and repair idempotent rather than introduce a separate transaction journal/queue. Exact CLI outcome and repair command should be grounded in existing doctor/update conventions. Current code writes Markdown first, then write_collection_displaynames can fail through bare atomic_write (filesystem/calendar.rs:1221-1249; cli/charter.rs:606-620), so it misleadingly returns a total error after a committed write. EffectBatch validates preconditions but executes sequentially; moving both writes into one batch would not guarantee rollback. Implement truthful partial outcome, detection and repair with failure-injection tests. Remaining mechanical review nits in this same action: validate_source_path in filesystem/mod.rs:276 must reject lexical traversal for missing targets, and write_charter_document has a dangling /// line.$ !4 ^2026-09-18T11:03 #01a0b5b0-17eb-7573-9921-dd40c4212d25
[ ] Give agent-facing verbs typed input and output\: show as JSON, text from stdin $NEEDS DECISION: which JSON shapes and which stdin/file flag convention should the agent-facing verbs use? The input half is decidable and ready (read note text from stdin or a file for add, update and jot); the output half is an agent-facing schema, which this project reserves for the user, and the repo does not pin it down. (a) show action --format json has exactly one canonical target: clearhead_core::to_schema_document, the specifications actions schema that read actions --format json already emits, so it conforms with no new schema. (b) show charter has NO canonical document schema -- specifications/schemas/charter_metadata.schema.json is the sidecar, not the charter -- and the repo already ships two inconsistent shapes: read charters --format json serializes MarkdownCharter with nested actions, while read actions --format json emits the canonical SchemaAction document. So show charter --format json must choose: reuse the shipped read charters object, serialize the domain Charter, or design a new canonical charter document shape. (c) The stdin/file flag needs a convention: existing file commands use the positional 'if not provided, reads from stdin' pattern, but a note is already a flag value, so the options are --note-file \[PATH|-] (optional value; omitted or - reads stdin), a sentinel on the existing flag (--description - , --append-description - , jot -), or a separate --description-file pair. Original finding follows unchanged. Found 2026-09-18. show action and show charter print tables only, so an agent cannot read an action back as data; and add or update take long notes as command-line arguments, so a note with backticks, dollar signs or brackets is fought through shell quoting and the grammar. Add a JSON output form to show, and let add, update and jot read the note from stdin or a file flag. This is the CLI half of the typed-input problem and benefits any harness with a shell. Related: the append-description and newline actions; do them as one pass over the note path.$ !3 ^2026-09-18T11:39 #01a0b5d0-da47-714b-9a4e-113403989cea
[ ] Sort platform decisions into per-repo DECISIONS.md files $Per the Where knowledge lives rules in CONTRIBUTING (smallest repo; repos follow the platform, never the reverse): go through platform docs/DECISIONS.md 1-41 and move each decision that binds only one repo into that repo. Delete it from the platform file with no pointer, and never reuse its number. Start with Decision 40 (clearhead-core only). Then split any platform doc that cites a moved decision: cross-repo parts stay, repo-specific parts move into that repo. Known case: docs/crate-merge-and-charter-identity.md cites 40.$ ^2026-09-22T21:21 #01a0cc7e-dc1c-7797-9b59-5f718d9e165f
[ ] Migrate heading-style charter log entries to dated lines $charters.md now defines a log entry as one list item led by its own ISO 8601 date. Rewrite the heading-grouped entries (### date + prose, e.g. agent-surface 2026-09-17, plus 2026-09-15 and 2026-09-10) as dated one-line entries; move any analysis they carry into the action or decision the line points at.$ ^2026-09-22T22:15 #01a0ccb0-82ab-74a9-8b9c-c75730082150
[ ] Audit remaining ephemeral charter ID export surfaces $Found 2026-09-22 while implementing 01a0cc7e. clearhead-core docs/DECISIONS.md Decision 1 says a charter without a declared document id must never publish its shell-minted join key. The scoped ids/JSON-LD read fix does not cover other surfaces: piped read charters defaults to format_charter(domain Charter) which emits id frontmatter even when none was declared; generic RDF projection in rdf/project.rs names every charter urn:uuid and publishes hasUUID in export/query outputs. Canonical read charters JSON is tracked separately by 01a0cbb5. Audit show/orient/query/export/native reads too, carry source identity provenance from the same load, and ensure anonymous representation or omit IDs consistently without mutating source. Add regression tests for an id-less document and a sidecar-adopted but undeclared id; do not treat a computed ID as durable.$ ^2026-09-23T09:55 #01a0cf31-8189-79f1-aa65-8f85aa9631f2
